  "red_failure_evidence": "Before the mailbox lib functions existed, calling `bee state handoff write/show/adopt` in a repo with a live workflow record had no way to resolve or use a per-workflow mailbox at all — every call fell through to the single legacy .bee/HANDOFF.json regardless of how many workflows existed, so two paused workflows collided on one file (exactly the coupling startFeature's own pre-existing comment at lib/state.mjs:1987-1990 named as a known gap 'until slice 3's per-workflow mailboxes land'). During implementation, a genuine red was hit and fixed: writeMailboxHandoff/adoptMailboxHandoff run their body under lock.mjs's withStoreLock, which is ASYNC — the first handler wiring called them without awaiting, so `bee state handoff write --kind pause ...` returned `{}` (JSON.stringify of an un-awaited Promise) instead of the record; test_bee_cli.mjs's existing state.handoff.write/adopt example rows (which now route through the mailbox because rootState already has a live workflow via msn-6/10's startFeature dual-write) caught this immediately: 3 failing rows ('expected a pause handoff, got {}', 'expected the carried planned-next identifiers, got {}', and adopt exiting 1 with 'state handoff adopt: undefined'). Fixed by making handleStateHandoffWrite/handleStateHandoffAdopt async and awaiting writeMailboxHandoff/adoptMailboxHandoff — all 289 test_bee_cli.mjs rows then passed.",
  "notes": "New tests (skills/bee-hive/templates/tests/test_cli_state.mjs, multisession-native-15 section, 8 rows): (1) two handoffs for one workflow keep distinct seq, second same-role write auto-clears the first, adopt picks the newest OPEN record; (2) target_role scoping — a reviewer-role handoff never clobbers the implementer/default-role handoff (distinct seq, independently open); a second reviewer write clears only the first reviewer record; (3) adoptMailboxHandoff bumps fence_epoch by exactly 1 (msn-12's adoptClaim) and a re-run after full clear is a typed NO_HANDOFF no-op (claim byte-unchanged, no re-bump); (4) crash-recovery self-heal (status 'adopted' but not yet 'cleared') completes the clear WITHOUT re-adopting the claim or re-bumping the epoch; (5) C1 legacy fallback — a repo with zero workflow records anywhere never creates .bee/runtime/handoffs/ and keeps writing the single legacy file, byte-identical result shape (no workflow_id/id/status/target_role/seq fields leak into the legacy record); (6) rebuildHandoffProjection mirrors the newest OPEN mailbox record (any workflow) into the legacy shape (writer_session present, mailbox-only fields dropped), removes the legacy file once no workflow has an open handoff, and readHandoff (the byte-identical readers' shared entry point) sees it unchanged; (7) invariant 9 end-to-end through the real CLI dispatcher: workflow A (default pipeline) pauses with an open handoff, workflow B starts as a lane WHILE A is paused (never blocked — F5 per-feature scoping), B writes and adopts its OWN planned-next handoff via --lane, and A's mailbox record is proven completely untouched (same seq, same status) throughout — plus `state handoff show` with no --lane still resolves A's pause (default record's own workflow) while `--lane <B>` shows B's now-cleared (null) state, proving per-session/per-workflow visibility. Byte-identical-reader suites re-run and green with ZERO edits to those files: hooks/test_hook_contracts.mjs (bee-session-close.mjs's readHandoff), scripts/test_compaction_advisories.mjs + scripts/test_compaction_module.mjs (compaction.mjs), and inject.mjs's buildSessionPreamble rows inside test_cli_state.mjs/test_misc.mjs — all pass because rebuildHandoffProjection keeps the legacy file a faithful mirror. Two known-drift tests updated as required maintenance, not scope creep: the lib/state.mjs export allowlist (test_misc.mjs) now lists the 7 new exports, and command-registry.mjs gained optional --lane/--target-role/--session-id properties on state.handoff.write/adopt/show so their --help --json schemas match the new resolution flags."
